WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.160
On July 24th, 2026, more than 270

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.600
companies and organizations signed an open

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.440
letter to Washington.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.520
Microsoft, Nvidia, Meta, and a long list

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.660
of others urged policymakers not to

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.260
restrict open-weight AI models.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.360
The letter is about policy, but it is

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.000
really a market signal.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.590
It is telling you where the profit is

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.490
moving in the AI economy, and it is not

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.750
staying in the models.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:27.510
That is the question we are going to

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:27.920
unpack.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.360
If capable AI models become open-weight,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.880
who keeps the profit?

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.200
Here is the short answer.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.520
Value migrates, it does not disappear.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.350
The model itself is becoming the cheapest

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.640
part of the stack.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.540
The money is moving to distribution,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.170
workflow ownership, proprietary context,

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:43.940
and trust.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.160
Start with the definition we need.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.340
An open-weight model is software you can

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.880
download, inspect, modify, and run on your

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.240
own infrastructure.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.870
That means the model is interchangeable.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.670
If you can run the same capability

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.000
anywhere, the model stops being a product

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.760
and becomes a raw input.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.330
The product is the system around it, the

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.490
workflow that turns the model into a

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.700
result, the data that makes the result

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.850
worth something, the distribution that

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.100
puts it in front of a user, and the trust

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.810
that keeps them coming back.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:14.930
Now follow the money.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.490
Gartner forecasts worldwide AI spending

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:22.450
will reach $2.59 trillion in 2026, up 47%

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.260
from a year earlier.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.880
But that spending is not landing evenly.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.600
The thing to watch is the price of a

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.500
token, the unit that AI companies sell.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.440
Token costs are plunging.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.140
Open-weight models are flooding the

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.430
market, and every model provider is being

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.700
forced to cut prices to keep up.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.200
That is the margin squeeze.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.140
The model layer is getting cheaper by the

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.090
quarter, while the layers around it

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.270
capture more of the value.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.340
Here is where the market comes in.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.720
Forbes reported in July that token costs

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.070
are falling so fast that model providers

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.850
face a new margin squeeze, while

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.470
enterprise buyers are shifting their

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.800
spending toward the application layer.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.920
The workflow and the data live there.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.640
That is the durable moat.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.400
Distribution is scarce.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.080
Workflow ownership is scarce.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.490
Proprietary context is scarce.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.650
The weights are not.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.000
So what is the honest uncertainty?

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.180
The uncertainty is not whether the model

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.600
gets commoditized.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.320
That is already happening.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.880
The uncertainty is which of the

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.010
surrounding layers turns out to be the

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.140
durable one.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.460
Distribution could consolidate around a

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.480
few platforms.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.510
Workflow could be owned by incumbents who

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.440
already sit inside the enterprise.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.560
Or new context advantages could emerge

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.410
from the data that only a specific

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.880
business can collect.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.500
No one knows which one compounds fastest.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.650
That is the bet.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.920
The signal to watch is not the benchmark

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.600
scores.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.660
It is the behavior underneath them.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.860
Watch whether enterprises keep paying for

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.420
model access or start running open weights

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.260
on their own infrastructure.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.420
Watch where the application vendors put

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.380
their margins.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.250
Watch whether the biggest winners are the

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.580
ones who own the workflow, the

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.970
distribution, and the data, rather than

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.180
the model itself.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.400
The AI race was about building the best

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:02.870
model.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.920
Then it was about the cheapest model.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.120
Now it is about the system that makes the

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.080
model useful.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.600
That is the tension.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.920
And in 2026, it has a number attached.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.360
$2.59 trillion of spending, a flood of

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.690
open weights, and a margin squeeze that is

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.530
telling you exactly where the value is

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:20.850
going.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.180
The question for the next phase of the AI

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.470
economy is not whether the models get

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.100
better.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.720
It is who owns the layers around them.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.170
The moat is the system, not the model.
